		<header>
			<h1>Who Pays for the AI Transition?</h1>
			<p><b>By Joseph Fleishman</b></p>
		</header>

		<p>Anxiety about artificial intelligence is hard to escape right now. It is in the news, in classrooms, and all over social media, where every new tool seems to arrive with a
promise and a warning attached. What once sounded like a distant Silicon Valley idea is now
actively changing how people think about work, security, and the future. The question is no longer
just whether AI will change jobs. It is which workers will be reorganized first, and who will have
enough power to shape the terms of that change.
</p>

		<p>That question matters because work has never been only about a paycheck. A job gives people
income, but it also gives them routine, status, identity, bargaining power, and a way to imagine
the future. In the United States, employment is tied to health care, housing, retirement, and social
worth in ways that make job insecurity feel personal almost immediately. When people worry
about automation, they are asking whether the rules that once made a stable life possible are being
rewritten without them.
</p>

		<p>To move past the headlines, it helps to look at the people actually shaping the argument. In a
recent <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/jamie-dimon-artificial-intelligence-work-week/">CBS
News interview</a>, Jamie Dimon, the CEO of JPMorgan Chase, described a future in which AI could
mean shorter workweeks, longer lives, and major medical breakthroughs. He made a similar point
three years earlier in a <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2023-10-02/dimon-says-ai-may-bring-3-day-workweek-in-the-future-video">
Bloomberg TV interview</a>, when he said future generations might work three and a half
days a week while maintaining high living standards. Dimon also told younger workers to focus on
skills that machines still struggle with, including communication, teamwork, and judgment. He
acknowledges disruption, and his version of the future assumes the gains can be shared widely
enough to make the disruption worth it.
</p>

		<p>That kind of optimism is common among executives who see AI first as a productivity tool.From the top of a large company, the technology looks like a way to remove friction, speed up analysis, and make organizations more efficient. It also fits a familiar American story: new machines arrive, old tasks disappear, new tasks emerge, and the economy finds another balance. The weaker part of the story is what happens in between. A technology can make an economy richer while leaving many workers less secure.</p>

		<p>Not all visions of the future come from boardrooms. On TikTok, Grimes claimed that <a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/danidiplacido/2021/06/04/on-tiktok-grimes-pitches-fully-automated-luxury-communism/">"AI is actually the fastest path to communism,"</a> imagining algorithms that could run farming, reduce corruption, and create abundance with less tedious work. She later said the video was partly a joke, but the idea stuck because it named a real desire. If machines can do more of the work, then people naturally ask whether life could become less punishing. The fantasy sounds exaggerated, but the frustration behind it is not hard to understand.</p>

		<p>The joke landed because many younger workers already feel that the economy has broken its promises. They came of age during rising housing costs, unstable job markets, heavy debt, and a weakening belief that education or effort will reliably lead to mobility. In that setting, a future with less tedious work and more freedom does not sound like a luxury. It sounds like a response to exhaustion. The meme worked because it made a serious grievance sound casual enough to circulate. It also showed how quickly labor politics now moves through internet culture, where a half-serious joke can express what a policy paper says more carefully: people want technology to give them time back, not just make employers more efficient.</p>

		<p>The sharpest warning now comes from inside the AI industry itself. Dario Amodei, the co-founder and CEO of Anthropic, told <a href="https://www.axios.com/2025/05/28/ai-jobs-white-collar-unemployment-anthropic">Axios</a> that AI companies and government should stop softening the possibility of serious white-collar job disruption. That warning complicates the usual divide between tech boosters and tech critics, because it comes from someone building one of the major AI systems at the center of the debate. Amodei has also written a more optimistic essay, <a href="https://darioamodei.com/essay/machines-of-loving-grace">"Machines of Loving Grace,"</a> imagining AI as a force that could improve health, science, and economic life. Taken together, those two positions make him useful here: he shows how the same industry can sell abundance while warning that the transition may be brutal.</p>

		<p>Andrew Yang brings the argument back to policy. The entrepreneur and former 2020 Democratic presidential candidate built much of his political identity around the warning that automation could displace millions of workers, especially people in routine or repetitive jobs. His presidential campaign centered on the Freedom Dividend, a universal basic income proposal meant to soften automation shocks before they spread through the economy. In a recent newsletter, he updated the same argument for the generative AI era with a shorter slogan: <a href="https://blog.andrewyang.com/p/tax-the-bots">tax the bots</a>.</p>

		<p>If machines help companies replace or reduce human labor, Yang argues, then the tax system should capture part of that value and send it back into the economy. The idea is controversial, but it asks a concrete question that the broader AI debate often avoids: if automation creates wealth while weakening the link between work and income, who should pay for the transition?</p>

		<p>Curtis Yarvin represents a very different reaction. Yarvin, a software developer and techno-libertarian political writer who publishes under the name Mencius Moldbug, <a href="https://graymirror.substack.com/p/there-is-no-ai-risk">has argued</a> that much of the fear around AI resembles a modern "golem" myth more than a realistic forecast. His point was that intelligence by itself does not become power. Machines still operate through institutions, owners, incentives, and commands. That shifts the focus away from the machine as a monster and toward the people and organizations deciding where the machine gets used.</p>

		<p>Yarvin's argument is useful even for those who reject his politics because it shows how much ideology shapes the AI debate. For some people, AI risk is mainly a labor story about displacement, inequality, and declining security. For others, panic about AI inflates the technology and the status of the people building it. The disagreement is partly technical, but it is also a fight over power. Different groups are arguing about whose fear counts as realistic and whose fear gets dismissed as hysteria.</p>

		<figure class="people">
			<div class="person"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jamie%20Dimon%2C%20CEO%20of%20JPMorgan%20Chase.jpg"><b>Jamie Dimon</b></div>
			<div class="person"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Grimes%20%28cropped%29.jpg"><b>Grimes</b></div>
			<div class="person"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dario%20Amodei%20at%20TechCrunch%20Disrupt%202023%2001%20%28cropped%29.jpg"><b>Dario Amodei</b></div>
			<div class="person"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Andrew%20Yang%20by%20Gage%20Skidmore.jpg"><b>Andrew Yang</b></div>
			<div class="person"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Curtis%20Yarvin%20portrait%20%283x4%20cropped%29.jpg"><b>Curtis Yarvin</b></div>
		</figure>

		<p>Ultimately, what ties these voices together is the sense that the old rules are under pressure. Dimon imagines AI as a productivity engine, Grimes turns abundance into internet fantasy, Amodei warns that the builders themselves may be moving faster than society can absorb, Yang asks who should pay for the disruption, and Yarvin doubts that the machine itself is the real center of power. The disagreement is not just about whether AI is impressive. It is about who controls the transition and who is left exposed by it. That is where the data becomes useful, because it moves the argument away from speculation and toward a more specific labor story.</p>

		<p>The data is simplified, as any occupational-risk model has to be, but the pattern is still clear. The categories are occupational, so the forecast shows where exposure is likely to concentrate rather than exactly who will lose work. That matters because the outside debate is crowded with sweeping claims, while this analysis is narrower: it asks where exposure appears inside this dataset. The point is not that some jobs are doomed and others are safe forever. The point is that the first wave of pressure is likely to be uneven.</p>

		<p>That unevenness matters because labor markets do not experience disruption as an average. They experience it through particular occupations, neighborhoods, age groups, and households with different amounts of savings and leverage. Public conversation often treats automation as if it will sweep across the whole economy at once. The occupational pattern suggests something more targeted. AI pressure follows the places where employers already have reasons to cut labor costs, monitor performance, and standardize tasks.</p>

		<p>That means broad claims about the future of work can hide the actual people who will feel the pressure first. The costs will not be evenly distributed just because the technology is widely discussed. A national productivity gain can still arrive as a local layoff, a reduced schedule, or a new software system that makes a job faster and less humane.</p>

		<figure class="graph-box">
			<iframe title="Risk category by sector" id="datawrapper-chart-TSGM9" src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/TSGM9/1/" height="289"></iframe>
		</figure>

		<p>When the data is broken out by sector, the hierarchy becomes hard to miss. Service work appears especially exposed, with most of those jobs falling into the high-risk category, while skilled trades also show substantial vulnerability. Healthcare and education remain more insulated because so much of their work depends on licensed expertise, care, trust, and responsibility. Business and legal work sit between those poles. Risk follows organizational power as much as technical possibility. The sectors with the weakest ability to resist efficiency mandates are often the same sectors where employers can describe work in the cleanest units: calls answered, packages moved, shifts filled, transactions completed, tasks closed.</p>

		<p>The most vulnerable work is usually built around repetition, routines, and tasks that can be divided into predictable steps. The safer work often depends on expertise, authority, care, or forms of interaction that remain difficult to standardize at scale. That does not make those jobs permanently safe. It means they are less likely to be the first target. Timing matters because workers hit first often have the least room to prepare.</p>

		<p>A worker who loses hours before a public retraining program exists is not living through a smooth transition. They are living through a gap between the speed of corporate adoption and the slower pace of public support. This is why public anxiety around AI becomes concrete once the discussion moves from broad sectors to actual jobs. A cashier, customer-support worker, or logistics employee does not occupy the same position as a doctor, teacher, or lawyer, even if all of them may eventually use AI tools.</p>

		<p>The difference lies in how easily a role can be turned into a system that managers can monitor, standardize, and scale. Once that conversion becomes possible, economic pressure gives employers a reason to try it. The worker may still be present, but the job can become more scripted, more surveilled, and more tightly measured until the human part of the work is treated as a narrow leftover.</p>

		<p>The pressure is landing in parts of the labor market that were already exposed before generative AI became a household phrase. The highest-risk jobs often have thinner margins of security, weaker bargaining power, lower prestige, and less control over schedules or working conditions. These are also the jobs where efficiency metrics have long dominated discussions of labor. AI gives firms a new way to pursue reductions they were already inclined to seek. The technology intensifies an existing logic instead of inventing one from scratch. That is why a cashier or support worker may hear an executive talk about innovation and hear something more immediate: fewer hours, tighter scripts, more surveillance, or a manager expecting the same staff to cover more ground.</p>

		<p>That history helps explain why the debate feels so charged. Automation is arriving in a labor market that was already unequal, and many workers suspect the new technology will deepen those divides unless policy interrupts the pattern. That suspicion is not anti-technology. It comes from experience. Previous waves of innovation raised productivity while leaving many households with less security than the aggregate numbers implied. The question is whether the people exposed to the costs of innovation have any real claim on its rewards.</p>

		<p>From the top of the economic ladder, AI can look exciting, efficient, and even liberating. From the edge of the labor market, it can look like another force pushing people toward replacement or downward mobility. Savings, credentials, professional networks, and organizational authority all change how a person experiences technological disruption. A worker with a financial cushion can treat reskilling as a challenge. A worker living paycheck to paycheck may experience the same transition as a threat to rent, health care, or family stability.</p>

		<figure class="graph-box">
			<iframe title="Automation probability inside tech" id="datawrapper-chart-wLI9a" src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/wLI9a/1/" height="430"></iframe>
		</figure>

		<p>The technology industry complicates the picture. It is often treated as the safest corner of the economy, the place where the winners of the AI boom are already gathering. The role-level data tells a more uneven story. Workers closest to AI development hold a stronger position than workers whose digital labor can be assisted, streamlined, or partially absorbed by the systems they use. Even the industry identified with the future contains a labor hierarchy.</p>

		<p>The clearest divide appears between jobs at the center of AI development and jobs positioned slightly farther away. AI engineers and research scientists appear more insulated because they help design, evaluate, or direct the systems reshaping other work. Software engineers, data scientists, graphic designers, and UX researchers are less exposed than service workers, though they do not occupy the same protected position as the roles closest to AI infrastructure. Technical skill matters, while a worker's place in the AI supply chain may matter just as much.</p>

		<p>The word "tech" has become too vague to explain what is happening. It is used as shorthand for innovation, prestige, security, wealth, and power all at once. Breaking the sector into specific roles makes the hierarchy clearer. Some workers are treated as architects of the transition. Others are close enough to automation that parts of their labor can be absorbed into tools, workflows, or smaller teams.</p>

		<p>That internal split challenges the assumption that high-status digital work is automatically future-proof. For years, software, design, and data work were often presented as safer paths through the labor market. The data suggests a more conditional form of security. A role is safer when it is strategically central and harder to reduce to repeatable output. It is more exposed when AI can change staffing models, team size, or expectations for how much one worker should produce.</p>

		<p>The risk may show up first as acceleration rather than unemployment: fewer entry-level openings, more output expected from smaller teams, and less patience for the slower learning curve that used to train new workers. Someone building AI systems has a different position from someone whose design, coding, or analytical work is increasingly supported by those systems. Salary and education matter, though the structure of the task may matter more when employers decide where automation can save money.</p>

		<p>The difference may look subtle now, but it can become larger over time. A worker whose role is close to the design of AI systems has a different kind of leverage than a worker expected to use those systems under higher output targets. General digital fluency may not be enough if the job itself can be folded into a tool, a workflow, or a smaller team. That is why the safest-looking parts of the labor market can still contain their own hierarchy of risk.</p>

		<p>The AI conversation also reveals something about status. Public debate easily accepts that machines may threaten routine labor, especially when that labor is already undervalued. It is harder to admit how many jobs were treated as routine long before AI could automate pieces of them. The technology inherits that hierarchy and gives it a new technical language.</p>

		<figure class="graph-box">
			<iframe title="Occupations across the risk spectrum" id="datawrapper-chart-sQSaX" src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/sQSaX/1/" height="470"></iframe>
		</figure>

		<p>Across occupations, the highest-risk roles read less like a neutral labor forecast and more like a map of class exposure. Retail workers, security guards, construction workers, customer-support workers, and truck drivers do different jobs, yet much of their work can be described in routines, task boundaries, and outputs that companies can imagine optimizing, outsourcing, or partially automating. Their vulnerability comes from the organization of the work as much as from the capabilities of AI.</p>

		<p>The exposure is not identical in every job. Customer support can be automated through chatbots and scripts. Retail work can be changed through self-checkout, scheduling software, and inventory systems. Trucking and construction involve physical work, but companies can still use software to route, monitor, coordinate, or reduce parts of the labor process. The shared pattern is not that every job disappears in the same way. It is that employers can imagine squeezing more output from fewer people.</p>

		<p>The consequences would also be regional and local. Many towns and neighborhoods depend on service, logistics, retail, construction, and transportation jobs like these. A change that looks efficient from a corporate office can mean lost hours at a store, fewer routes for a driver, tighter quotas in a warehouse, or fewer reliable jobs in a community that already has limited alternatives.</p>

		<p>That local effect matters because job loss is rarely only an individual event. When a large category of work becomes less stable, the pressure moves through rent payments, family budgets, small businesses, and public services. The economy may call it adjustment, but workers experience it as a change in daily life.</p>

		<p>What stands out is where these jobs already sat in the economy before AI entered the picture. They often carried less prestige, weaker security, thinner margins for error, and greater physical or emotional strain. A professional worker may experience AI as software that removes a tedious task. A lower-wage worker may experience it as the reason a supervisor cuts a shift, raises a quota, or decides that a smaller staff can handle the same workload. That is why the automation debate feels moral as well as economic.</p>

		<p>Lower-risk work follows a different pattern. Doctors, nurses, teachers, AI engineers, and research scientists depend more heavily on judgment, care, expertise, trust, or public responsibility. AI may still alter diagnosis, lesson planning, research workflows, and administrative work. Those roles sit farther from the immediate pressure to replace workers with systems because society still attaches public responsibility and professional authority to them.</p>

		<p>Automation is entering a labor market that was already unequal. The jobs most exposed to automation are often the jobs least able to demand protection on their own. That imbalance turns a technology story into a political story. The distribution of risk will depend on unions, regulation, public investment, employer choices, and the willingness to treat vulnerable workers as worth protecting before the damage becomes visible in layoffs and reduced hours.</p>

		<p>When a category of work is displaced or downgraded, the damage is financial and social at the same time. A layoff, wage cut, or reduction in hours also carries a judgment about whose labor is considered irreplaceable and whose work can be optimized away. In the United States, employment remains one of the main ways people prove usefulness, stability, and belonging. When work loses value, the consequences spread into family life, local economies, mental health, and political trust.</p>

		<p>Automation debates keep drifting into moral language because they are arguments about obligation as much as efficiency. If society benefits from labor-saving technology, it has to decide what it owes the people whose labor becomes less necessary. Minimal support turns innovation into abandonment for those who absorb the shock. Stronger wages, benefits, training, bargaining power, redistribution, and attention to job quality would make the same technology feel very different. The moral question is also practical, because workers who experience change as abandonment are less likely to trust the institutions asking them to adapt.</p>

		<p>The broader economic history sharpens the point. Decades earlier, Robert Solow, the Nobel Prize-winning economist associated with the study of economic growth, <a href="https://www.standupeconomist.com/pdf/misc/solow-computer-productivity.pdf">observed that the computer age seemed to appear everywhere except in the productivity statistics</a>. The paradox was eventually resolved when productivity surged in the late 1990s, but the gains did not arrive evenly. Wages for many workers at the bottom and middle moved slowly while returns at the top accelerated. The lesson for the AI era is direct: productivity growth and shared prosperity are separate outcomes. That history should make leaders cautious when they promise that a more efficient economy will automatically become a fairer one. The distribution has to be built; it does not appear on its own.</p>

		<figure>
			<img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Robert%20Solow%20by%20Olaf%20Storbeck.jpg">
			<b>Robert Solow</b>
		</figure>

		<p>The most plausible danger is probably not a sudden collapse of the whole labor market. A more likely outcome is a sorting process that rewards workers whose skills complement machines while pressuring workers whose jobs can be described as a sequence of steps. AI can create value without creating broadly shared value. It can raise efficiency without making households more secure. That distinction should matter more than dramatic predictions about total job loss.</p>

		<p>Policy matters more than hype here. If the pressure falls hardest on service work, logistics, and the trades, support has to begin there before displacement becomes a crisis. Stronger labor protections, wage insurance, portable benefits, better training systems, public investment, and redistribution all belong in the conversation. Yang's warning is useful if it forces policy makers to plan before the shock arrives. Dimon's optimism becomes realistic only if societies deliberately distribute the gains instead of assuming they will spread on their own. A serious response would also measure job quality, because a labor market can preserve employment while degrading schedules, wages, autonomy, and the predictability people need to build stable lives.</p>

		<p>Counting jobs alone would miss much of the damage. A person can remain employed while losing hours, predictability, autonomy, or the chance to move up. A company can avoid layoffs and still make work feel more temporary, more monitored, and less stable. If AI changes the terms of work in that way, the headline employment number will understate the disruption people actually feel.</p>

		<p>Technology does not decide whether change becomes liberating or brutal. Institutions, markets, management decisions, and public policy turn technical capacity into lived reality. Bargaining power matters because workers with leverage can shape how tools are adopted. Workers without leverage often experience the same tools as commands from above. If AI widens inequality, the cause will include the technology, but it will also include the weakness of the response around it.</p>

		<p>The data cannot predict the future with certainty. Transitions are uneven, and labor markets are too complex for that kind of precision. Still, the direction of pressure is visible. Some workers will benefit from AI early, especially those whose judgment and authority are strengthened by the tools. Others will absorb disruption through reduced hours, tighter monitoring, lower bargaining power, or displacement. The central question is who pays for that change, and whether the gains are shared widely enough to make the transition defensible. The answer will come from the political choices made while the technology is still new enough for its rules to be contested.</p>

